# Lesson 4, new exercise: double finesse in a trump contract. AJT9 in dummy opposite 632; lead toward dummy twice, losing at most one diamond, and the fourth diamond takes care of a heart loser.
title: Two finesses in diamonds
id: L04-20
type: exercise
lesson: 4
concepts: 4.5
student: S
board: 74
dealer: E
vulnerable: both

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  J963   J62    AJT9   84
  E  42     T753   K87    T975
  S  AKQ85  AK4    632    A2
  W  T7     Q98    Q54    KQJ63

auction:
  E Pass
  S 1S     = 13+ points, at least four spades
     not *: 20 points and five spades: open your long suit at the one level.
  W Pass
  N 2S     = 6-9 points, 3+ spades
  E Pass
  S 4S     = Game
     not *: You have 20 points, and partner has at least 6: enough for game.
  W Pass
  N Pass
  E Pass

auction recap:
  You opened 1♠ and partner raised to 2♠. With 20 points, you bid game. You
  are declarer, because you bid spades first, and so West will lead to the
  first trick.

planning:
  trumps out: 4
  losers: 4
  spades: 0 = Solid.
  hearts: 1 = Your third heart.
  diamonds: 2 = Dummy's ace takes care of one. The other two can shrink to one if you lead toward dummy's ace-jack-ten twice.
  clubs: 1 = Your second club.
  remind: You can afford three losers in 4♠. Count the obvious losers suit by suit in your own hand: spades, hearts, diamonds, clubs.
  remind: Dummy is missing the king and queen of diamonds. Which hand should lead diamonds, and how often?
play:
  1   W CK  N C4  E C5  S CA
      note: Four losers, one too many. Diamonds are the answer: lead toward dummy twice, and you lose only one diamond unless East has both the king and the queen. Then dummy's fourth diamond takes care of your heart loser. Win the ♣A.
  2   S S5  W S7  N SJ  E S2
      note: Draw trumps. Dummy's jack first, then back to your hand, ready to lead diamonds.
  3   N S3  E S4  S SA  W ST
      note: Trumps are drawn, and you're in your hand.
  4   S D2  W D4  N D9*  E DK
      note: Lead a diamond toward dummy.
      D9 key: West played low, so finesse. East can win this one, but you'll lead toward dummy again.
      D9 not DA: With the ace first, you can't trap both the king and the queen. Finesse now and finesse again later.
  5   E CT  S C2  W C3  N C8
      note: The first finesse lost to the king. Your club loser goes now.
  6   E H3  S HA  W H8  N H2
      note: Back to your hand to lead diamonds again.
  7   S D3  W D5  N DT*  E D7
      note: Lead toward dummy again.
      DT key: West played low again, so finesse again. If West has the queen, the ten wins.
      DT not DA: Did you notice West may still have the queen? Finesse again: you lose only if East has both honors.
  8   N DA  E D8  S D6  W DQ
      note: The ten won. Cash the ace.
  9   N DJ  E H5  S H4  W C6
      note: Dummy's last diamond takes care of your heart loser.
  10  N H6  E H7  S HK  W H9
  11  S SK  W CJ  N S6  E HT
      note: The rest are yours.
  12  S SQ  W CQ  N S9  E C9
  13  S S8  W HQ  N HJ  E C7
      note: 11 tricks: 4♠ made with an overtrick.

play recap:
  The plan: you counted four losers, one too many. Leading diamonds toward
  dummy twice would lose only one diamond, and dummy's fourth diamond would
  take care of your heart loser.

  The first finesse lost to East's king, but the second one won, because
  West held the queen. Leading toward dummy twice loses two diamonds only
  when East holds both the king and the queen, about one time in four.
